            "exercises": [
              {
                "label": "Exercise 1 — Steps in Preparing a Presentation",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What are the seven steps in preparing a presentation?",
                    "a": "1. Plan your presentation — organise content and practise delivery for a confident presentation. 2. Audience consideration — understand and engage the audience through simple language and visuals. 3. Clear purpose — keep a clear and engaging presentation purpose. 4. Content arrangement — structure content logically for better understanding. 5. Ideas arranged in bullet points — use concise bullet points to present key ideas. 6. Data in sequence — present facts and data in a logical order. 7. Simple and concise language — use clear and concise language for audience engagement."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Exercise 2 — Guidelines for Delivering a Presentation Effectively",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "What guidelines help in delivering a presentation effectively?",
                    "a": "1. Introduce the self and topic — introduce yourself clearly and concisely (who are you? Which class do you belong to? Why are you here?) and introduce the topic (what is it? Why did you select it? What is its purpose? How will it be useful to the audience?). 2. Present with clarity the main point or subject — explain the main point, arrange main points in logical sequence and explain each in detail, and clearly explain the key idea, purpose and scope. 3. Support the topic with effective factual information — provide relevant data, examples, statistics or research, and textual references or quotes from an editorial, article, journal or book. 4. Use appropriate etiquettes of interacting with the audience — show respect through polite language, active listening and engaging the audience; ask questions and share ideas in the middle of the presentation to keep them active. 5. Structure ideas and arguments in a coherent, logical manner — organise content in a clear sequence: introduction, main points, and conclusion. 6. Make appropriate use of audio-visual aids — use slides, images, videos, charts or props to reinforce key messages and maintain audience interest. 7. Maintain eye contact — connect with the audience by looking at them directly rather than reading from notes or slides. 8. Use rhetorical questions for the purpose of presentation — engage and provoke thoughts by asking questions that encourage critical thinking or reflection without expecting direct answers; ask questions to let the audience participate."
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "label": "Exercise 1 — Prepare and Deliver Presentations on the Given Topics",
                "items": [
                  {
                    "q": "1. Prepare a presentation on 'The Effects of Fast-Food Consumption on Health'. Give its outline.",
                    "a": "Introduction: I am [name], a student of Class XII. My topic is the effects of fast-food consumption on health — a habit that is harming our young generation. Main points: (1) Fast food is high in fat, salt and sugar and low in nutrition. (2) It causes obesity, diabetes, heart disease and weak immunity. (3) It affects concentration and energy in studies. (4) Healthy alternatives: home food, fruits, vegetables and water. Conclusion: Fast food may be tasty, but health is more precious — eat wisely and live healthily. (Use simple language, bullet points, pictures of healthy vs junk food, and eye contact.)"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "2. Prepare a presentation on 'Lack of Reading Culture among students'. Give its outline.",
                    "a": "Introduction: I am [name], a student of Class XII. My topic is the lack of reading culture among students — a problem of our mobile age. Main points: (1) Students spend hours on mobile phones and social media but read very few books. (2) Causes: easy entertainment, no library habit, and no encouragement at home. (3) Effects: weak vocabulary, poor writing, and shallow thinking. (4) Solutions: school libraries, reading competitions, and a daily reading hour. Conclusion: Readers are leaders — let us bring back the habit of reading. (Support with a short survey of classmates and a quote about books.)"
                  },
                  {
                    "q": "3. Prepare a presentation on 'Time Management Skill: a skill to ensure the success'. Give its outline.",
                    "a": "Introduction: I am [name], a student of Class XII. My topic is time management — the skill that ensures success. Main points: (1) Time is the most precious resource; once lost, it never returns. (2) Plan the day: make a timetable, set priorities, and do the hardest work first. (3) Avoid time-wasters: useless mobile use and delaying work. (4) Benefits: less stress, better results, and more free time. Conclusion: Those who manage time well manage life well. (Use a daily timetable chart as a visual aid and end with a rhetorical question: 'If you cannot manage an hour, how will you manage a life?')"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
