          {
            "exercises": [
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "Relativity is the theory of space, time and gravity developed by Albert Einstein. Special relativity deals with inertial frames and the constancy of the speed of light; general relativity deals with gravity as the curvature of spacetime.",
                    "q": "What is relativity? Who developed it?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The two postulates of special relativity are: (i) the laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames of reference; (ii) the speed of light in vacuum is the same in all inertial frames, independent of the motion of the source or observer.",
                    "q": "State the two postulates of special relativity."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "An inertial frame of reference is a frame at rest or moving with uniform velocity, in which Newton's laws hold. A non-inertial frame is an accelerating or rotating frame, in which fictitious forces appear.",
                    "q": "What are inertial and non-inertial frames of reference?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Time dilation is the slowing down of a moving clock as seen by a stationary observer: t = γt_0, where γ = 1/√(1 − v^2/c^2). A moving clock ticks more slowly than a clock at rest.",
                    "q": "What is time dilation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Length contraction is the shortening of a moving rod in the direction of its motion as seen by a stationary observer: L = L_0/γ. There is no contraction perpendicular to the motion.",
                    "q": "What is length contraction?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The Lorentz factor is γ = 1/√(1 − v^2/c^2). It is 1 at low speeds and becomes very large as v approaches c.",
                    "q": "What is the Lorentz factor γ?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The Michelson-Morley experiment (1887) tried to detect the ether wind — the motion of the Earth through the supposed ether. It found no difference in the speed of light in different directions, showing that the speed of light is constant and that the ether does not exist. It led Einstein to special relativity.",
                    "q": "What was the Michelson-Morley experiment? What did it show?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Special Relativity"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "The Galilean transformation is x′ = x − vt, t′ = t. It assumes time is absolute and velocities add simply. It works at low speeds but fails at speeds near c.",
                    "q": "What is the Galilean transformation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The Lorentz transformation is x′ = γ(x − vt), t′ = γ(t − vx/c^2). It replaces the Galilean transformation at high speeds and keeps the speed of light constant in all inertial frames.",
                    "q": "What is the Lorentz transformation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Simultaneity is relative: two events that are simultaneous in one inertial frame are not simultaneous in another frame moving relative to it. There is no absolute 'now' for all observers.",
                    "q": "What is the relativity of simultaneity?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The twin paradox: one twin travels in a fast spaceship and returns younger than the twin who stayed on Earth, because the travelling twin's clock runs slow (time dilation). It is not a real paradox because the travelling twin accelerates (changes frames) while the stay-at-home twin does not.",
                    "q": "What is the twin paradox?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The rest energy of a particle is E_0 = m_0c^2, the energy it has by virtue of its mass alone. For an electron it is 0.511 MeV; for a proton it is 938 MeV.",
                    "q": "What is rest energy? What is it for an electron?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The total energy of a moving particle is E = γm_0c^2, and its kinetic energy is K.E. = (γ − 1)m_0c^2. At low speeds this reduces to the classical (1/2)m_0v^2.",
                    "q": "What are the total energy and kinetic energy of a relativistic particle?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The speed of light in vacuum is c = 3×10^8 m/s. It is the maximum possible speed in the universe, and it is the same for all observers.",
                    "q": "What is the speed of light? Why is it special?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — Transformations and Mass-Energy"
              },
              {
                "items": [
                  {
                    "a": "General relativity describes gravity as the curvature of spacetime caused by mass and energy. Objects move along the straightest paths (geodesics) in curved spacetime, which we see as gravitational attraction.",
                    "q": "What is general relativity?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "The equivalence principle states that inertial mass and gravitational mass are equal, so an observer cannot distinguish between a gravitational field and an accelerated frame of reference.",
                    "q": "State the equivalence principle."
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Spacetime is the four-dimensional combination of the three dimensions of space and one dimension of time. In relativity, space and time are not separate; they form a single fabric that is curved by mass and energy.",
                    "q": "What is spacetime?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Gravitational lensing is the bending of light from a distant star by the gravity of a massive object (such as a galaxy) between the star and the observer. It can produce multiple images or rings (Einstein rings) of the distant object.",
                    "q": "What is gravitational lensing?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Gravitational time dilation is the slowing of time in a strong gravitational field: clocks run slower near a massive object than far from it. GPS satellites must correct for this effect.",
                    "q": "What is gravitational time dilation?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "A black hole is a region of spacetime where gravity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape from inside its event horizon. It is formed when a very massive star collapses.",
                    "q": "What is a black hole?"
                  },
                  {
                    "a": "Gravitational waves are ripples in spacetime produced by accelerating massive objects, such as merging black holes. They were predicted by Einstein in 1916 and first detected in 2015 by LIGO.",
                    "q": "What are gravitational waves? When were they first detected?"
                  }
                ],
                "label": "Additional Questions — General Relativity"
              }
            ],
            "name": "Additional Questions",
            "num": "10.3"
          }
